.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt BASE64 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm base64
.Nd encode or decode base64 data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl di
.Op Fl w Ar columns
.Op Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Encode input bytes with the standard base64 alphabet and write standard
output.
Input is one named file, or standard input when omitted or named
.Ql - .
Encoding wraps lines at 76 characters by default.
.Fl w Ar columns
changes that width; zero disables wrapping.
.Fl d
selects decoding.
Decoding accepts newlines in the encoded input;
.Fl i
also ignores other non-alphabet bytes.
.Pp
Invalid encoded input returns failure and may already have emitted a
partial decoded stream.
Check status before treating a redirected output file as complete.
Encoding provides no encryption, authenticity or checksum.
The input file is unchanged; shell redirection, if used, owns output
creation and truncation.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 after successful conversion; nonzero for invalid input, options
or I/O failure. Diagnostics use standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
base64 /etc/alpha.txt
base64 /etc/alpha.txt | base64 -d
.Ed
.Sh ABSD PROFILE
This is the unmodified uutils 0.12.0 utility pinned by the platform build.
The reference describes selected source-supported options; use
.Fl Fl help
for the complete upstream interface.
File access uses the process's ABSD filesystem view, output uses its
inherited stream, and the command acquires no additional authority.
There are no native Unix processes, signals or permission bits underneath
this port.
The command matrix exercises the example forms below; other options
listed here are source-checked, not separately guest-qualified.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cat 1 ,
.Xr sha256sum 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
